WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
The delta theorem is the independent
arithmetic branch of our proof. We allow

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.400
Gaussian integer coefficients because Node
three's polynomial g already has them.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.260
The result applies to any polynomial
with the required norm identity,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.720
beyond the Hadamard construction.
Begin with nine cyclic positions

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.190
and a Gaussian integer polynomial f
whose product with its star is nine.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.580
Substitute any ninth root of unity.
Star becomes complex conjugation,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.820
so the squared absolute value of the
output is nine. Every output lies

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.220
on a circle of radius three. These output
values are different from the input roots.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.560
For a concrete example, take
f of P equal to three i P.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.970
Its star is negative three i P
inverse, and their product is nine.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.910
Each input rotates ninety
degrees and scales by three.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.220
The nine outputs are equally
spaced for this particular example.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:57.020
It is a Gaussian group ring
polynomial, not a Hadamard sign row.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.480
Compare the outputs at one and omega,
a primitive cube root of unity.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.940
Define delta of f as f of one divided
by f of omega. Their magnitudes cancel.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.600
The quotient records the numerator's
phase minus the denominator's phase:

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.610
the rotation from f of omega to f of one.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.640
For our example, those angles are
ninety and two hundred ten degrees.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.740
Their difference is negative
one hundred twenty degrees.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.580
Thus delta is one over
omega, or omega squared.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.240
Turn clockwise from the
denominator to the numerator.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.440
Copy that same rotation onto the positive
real direction of a separate unit circle.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.400
The quotient sits there, not at
either original output point.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.540
For general odd u greater than one,
let I be its distinct prime divisors.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.720
Choose a primitive pth
root for each prime p.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.500
For a subset S, multiply its
chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.320
the empty subset gives one. Every such
input raised to u squared equals one.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.380
Assume f has Gaussian integer
coefficients and product with

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.010
its star equal to u squared.
Every evaluation is nonzero.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.940
Define delta by multiplying
evaluations from even sized

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.860
subsets and dividing by
those from odd sized subsets.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.660
The empty subset supplies
f of one in the numerator.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.440
With u equal to three, this
is our original quotient.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.440
With two distinct primes p and q,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.520
the numerator is f of one times
f at the product of their roots.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.820
The denominator is f at the pth
root times f at the qth root.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.420
This is a ratio of ratios:
phases add and subtract.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.680
Repeated powers of one prime
create no new subset direction.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.520
Why does this product have finite order?

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.320
Kronecker's theorem says that an
algebraic integer whose algebraic

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.000
conjugates all have absolute
value one is a root of unity.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.430
The norm identity gives every
conjugate of delta absolute value one:

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.980
equal counts of numerator and denominator
factors cancel their magnitudes.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.960
An arithmetic comparison of the
evaluations supplies algebraic

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.060
integrality. Together, these two
inputs allow Kronecker's theorem.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.360
A separate congruence argument at
the odd primes shows that this root

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.160
of unity has odd order. Kronecker alone
does not supply that extra conclusion.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.500
For the nine position case, the
full argument leaves just one,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.240
omega, or omega squared as
the possible values of delta.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:42.100
Return to c, d, and g. Each has product
with its star equal to u squared,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.040
so the theorem makes all three delta
values roots of unity of odd order.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.120
Carry this arithmetic input to Node six.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.120
The other branch brings congruences
inherited from their common signs.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.480
Those are different ingredients,
which we will combine next.
